{
  "image_name": "5607728acd41da60db163227818f72f4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual progression showing how organic matter (OM) changes iron-bearing minerals from largely redox-inert to increasingly redox-active, and eventually to mineral transformation. At first, clay minerals and crystalline iron oxides appear to have little Fe redox cycling because nutrient and electron limitations block microbial activity. With OM complexation, microorganisms and OM interact with iron minerals, forming Fe–OM associations and enabling electron and atom exchange. This makes the minerals redox-active.\n\nAs OM effects increase, the minerals seem to support stronger electron transfer behavior, labeled as electron shuttling, buffering, and conducting. Different pathways of enhanced redox cycling are indicated, suggesting progressively greater coupling between iron phases and electron flow. The overall trend is an increase in Fe redox cycling extent and rate from left to right.\n\nIn the final stage, OM has combined effects on iron minerals, leading not just to redox activity but to phase transformation: a crystalline iron oxide is shown converting toward an amorphous iron oxide form. The message of the figure is that OM can activate otherwise sluggish iron minerals, facilitate microbial and mineral electron exchange, and ultimately drive structural changes in Fe mineral phases.",
